### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### LIVE DEMO 1: Predict, Then Run (slide 16)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### Q1. Every book (200, an array)
GET http://localhost:3000/books

### Q2. One book: The Firm (200)
GET http://localhost:3000/books/0099826704

### Q3. No such ISBN (404)
GET http://localhost:3000/books/0000000000

### Q4 is in the Node REPL:  parseInt('0099826704')  ->  99826704
### Optional, the same mistake inside a route (BAD route B2): 404 for a book that exists
GET http://localhost:3000/parseintbookdemo/0099826704


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### GLUE IT IN? (slide 13): SQL injection
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### BAD route B1, ISBN glued into the SQL: 200 and the first book in the table
GET http://localhost:3000/getbookdemo/' OR '1'='1

### GOOD route A2, ISBN bound: the same text is just a value, so 404
GET http://localhost:3000/books/' OR '1'='1


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### LIVE DEMO 2: With and Without a Transaction (slide 24)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### Q1. BAD route B3, no transaction, author 999 does not exist: 500 ...
###     ... but the Book row is left behind. Show it with section 2 of the .sql, then delete it.
POST http://localhost:3000/postbookdemo
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isbn": "0000000004",
  "title": "Should Not Exist",
  "yearPublish": "2026",
  "publisherId": 1,
  "bookCat": null,
  "authorId": 999
}

### Q2. GOOD route A3, same request inside a transaction: 500 and NO Book row
POST http://localhost:3000/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isbn": "0000000004",
  "title": "Should Not Exist",
  "yearPublish": "2026",
  "publisherId": 1,
  "bookCat": null,
  "authorId": 999
}

### Prove Q2: 404, the rollback undid the Book row too
GET http://localhost:3000/books/0000000004

### Q3. A real author: 201, one row in Book and one in BookAuthor (section 3 of the .sql)
POST http://localhost:3000/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isbn": "0000000002",
  "title": "Lab Test Book",
  "yearPublish": "2026",
  "publisherId": 1,
  "bookCat": null,
  "authorId": 1
}

### Q4. PUT on an ISBN that does not exist (404)
PUT http://localhost:3000/books/0000000099
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Nobody", "yearPublish": "2026", "publisherId": 1, "bookCat": null }

### Q4 contrast: the same PUT on the book from Q3 (200)
PUT http://localhost:3000/books/0000000002
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Lab Test Book, 2nd Edition", "yearPublish": "2026", "publisherId": 1, "bookCat": null }


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### COMPOSITE KEYS and CHECK (slides 28 to 30). Uses test book 0000000003.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### GET one copy (200)
GET http://localhost:3000/books/0000000003/copies/1

### A copy that does not exist (404)
GET http://localhost:3000/books/0000000003/copies/9

### CHECK demo: predict first. 1.00 is below 2.50: 500, the terminal names the CHECK constraint
PUT http://localhost:3000/books/0000000003/copies/1
Content-Type: application/json

{ "rentalRate": 1.00 }

### Then 3.50: 200 and the updated row
PUT http://localhost:3000/books/0000000003/copies/1
Content-Type: application/json

{ "rentalRate": 3.50 }

### DELETE one copy (204). Copy 2 survives: GET it afterwards.
DELETE http://localhost:3000/books/0000000003/copies/1


### ---------------------------------------------------------------------
### LIVE DEMO 3: One, Many or None (slide 36)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------

### Q1. Author 3: several books (200)
GET http://localhost:3000/authors/3/books

### Q2. Author 99: no books, so 200 and [], not 404
GET http://localhost:3000/authors/99/books


